;; db: bench_pathdriver
;; THE DATA-BLIND DRIVER PATHOLOGY (acyclic, 2 joins -- neither WCOJ nor
;; semijoin applies).  A simple path query X -> hub -> Z filtered by a tiny
;; selective relation `sel` on X:
;;
;;   sel(X,_)   3 rows          (the selective endpoint)
;;   big1(X,H)  N rows, H := 0   (every X into the single hub)
;;   big2(H,Z)  N rows, H  = 0   (the hub out to every Z)
;;
;; The good plan drives from sel (3 rows) and probes big1 then big2: O(N).
;; The bad plan drives from a big relation and applies sel LAST, after
;; big1 |><| big2 has enumerated the N*N (X,hub,Z) intermediate: O(N^2).
;;
;; The rule join planner (compiler/join-planning.rkt) is DATA-BLIND: with
;; all three relations arity 2 the structural score ties, and the driver is
;; chosen by source-clause order, not by size.  So the same query written
;; two ways picks opposite drivers and differs ~100x-260x at N=8k-16k, and
;; the gap WIDENS with N.  WCOJ3's join3 does not fire (acyclic, no cycle);
;; the semijoin filter does not fire either (it needs >= 3 join clauses --
;; this has 2).  Both maskers are inapplicable, so the bad driver stands
;; even in the default configuration.
;;
;; This spelling writes `sel` LAST, which happens to get the fast
;; practice" -- and gets the SLOW plan.  Swap the body to end with `sel` and
;; it gets the fast plan.  That fragility is the bug: a cardinality-aware
;; driver choice would drive from sel(3) regardless of how it is written.
table (big1 int int)
table (big2 int int)
table (sel int int)
table (ans int int)

rule (big1 X H) (big2 H Z) (sel X D) --> (ans X Z)
